  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 PM EDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nA lot of rain fell earlier in eastern portions of Knox County and \nsurrounding locales, with upwards of 3-4 inches in a narrow band \nfrom this morning's heaviest and steadiest rain. A handful of \nshowers are ongoing now and a couple of additional showers, or even \na storm, could develop late this afternoon into the early evening \nhours, though activity will remain far more isolated versus the \nwidespread coverage from this morning.\n\nIn response to the approaching boundary tomorrow and better upper\nlevel support, we should see a reflourishing of the rain shield \non Sunday, mainly south of I-40 and and south of I-81. This will \nyield an additional half inch pretty broadly, though if upper \nsupport helps, CAMs indicate potential for another narrow strip of\nup to 2\" of rainfall. It seems improbable this will fall on this \nmorning's super soaked areas, so again flood risk continues to be \non the low end. Rain rates should also generally remain on the low\nside, except if we see some thunderstorm development along the \nleading edge of activity, much like the leading edge this morning \nhad the heaviest rates. Since the atmosphere is quite saturated \nvertically, yielding low MLCAPE, I am not expecting any severe \nweather with the still to come rain, despite the marginally \nsupportive wind shear.\n\nNext week there's broad troughing way up north in Canada, broad \nridging out in the American West, and a rather benign pattern \nlocally. No significant height departures should mean pleasantly \nwarm temperatures and dry weather will reign supreme for much, if\nnot all, of the work week.",
